From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 04:03:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: apple: hold ANS's APCIe domains through a T8132
 sleep

The internal host's domains are held on through suspend-to-idle with a
genpd power-off veto. Hold them the same way through a T8132
suspend-to-RAM when apple-s2r says its sequence owns the block
(apple_sleep_apcie_hold()): genpd then neither gates them at noirq under
a running ANS, nor leaves them off on its books for the resume's resync
to gate the block the sequence raised. The hold ends in resume_noirq as
before.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 drivers/pci/controller/pcie-apple.c | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/pcie-apple.c b/drivers/pci/controller/pcie-apple.c
index b2ba1d3472..fb1d9896c6 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/pcie-apple.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/pcie-apple.c
@@ -5193,7 +5193,12 @@ static int apple_pcie_suspend_noirq(struct device *dev)
 	}
 	if (!pcie->hw->tunneled) {
 		apple_pcie_t8132_save(pcie);
-		if (pm_suspend_no_platform() && pcie->num_pds)
+		/*
+		 * A T8132 sleep lowers and raises ANS's block itself, after ANS is
+		 * stopped: the domains stay on until then, and on genpd's books after.
+		 */
+		if ((pm_suspend_no_platform() || apple_sleep_apcie_hold(pcie->dev->of_node)) &&
+		    pcie->num_pds)
 			return apple_pcie_hold_domains(pcie);
 		return 0;
 	}
